/**
 * people, long-form SEO overrides
 */
module.exports = {
  "jaimegarcia": "<p><strong>Jaime García</strong> is the blender who runs the Nicaraguan factory of <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a> in Estelí, the family company of his father, <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a>. The brand My Father began as Jaime's blend for Pepín.</p>\n<p>In Cuba Jaime studied agronomy and ran quality control in a state factory. After seeing <em>Cigar Aficionado</em> in 1997 he pushed the family to leave. His sister Janny went first. Pepín and Jaime followed, then opened <a href=\"/node/elreyhabanos/\">El Rey de los Habanos</a> in Miami in 2003 with Janny. <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a> was the first private-label client.</p>\n<p>In 2008 Jaime blended a cigar in secret to honor his father. Pepín found out, tasted it and approved it. That blend became My Father, and the Estelí factory that opened in 2009 took the same name. Jaime is the public blender of The Judge (2016; <em>Cigar Aficionado</em> Cigar of the Year 2024) and a core partner on Flor de las Antillas (Cigar of the Year 2012) and Le Bijou 1922 (Cigar of the Year 2015). Pepín remains the patriarch.</p>\n<p>Under Jaime the factory also rolls for others: Tatuaje, <a href=\"/node/latelier/\">L'Atelier</a>, <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a>, and Ashton's Nicaraguan brands, <a href=\"/node/laaromadecuba/\">La Aroma de Cuba</a> and San Cristóbal. My Father is a family business, not part of a tobacco conglomerate.</p>",
  "pepin": "<p><strong>José \"Pepín\" García</strong> is the Cuban-born cigar maker whose family runs <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a> and its factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. Born in 1950, he rolled his first cigar at 11 in his uncles' factories in Báez, Villa Clara, and spent decades in Cuba's cigar trade before leaving the island in 2001.</p>\n<p>In 2003 he opened <a href=\"/node/elreyhabanos/\">El Rey de los Habanos</a>, a small factory on Calle Ocho in Miami's Little Havana, with his son <a href=\"/node/jaimegarcia/\">Jaime</a> and daughter Janny. Its first private-label client was Pete Johnson's <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a>, and the family launched its own Don Pepín García cigars the same year. García also rolled for <a href=\"/node/padilla/\">Padilla</a> from 2005 until 2008, when demand from his other brands forced him to stop.</p>\n<p>Demand moved the family to Nicaragua. In 2008 Jaime blended the My Father cigar as a tribute to Pepín, and the new factory in Estelí took the name, formally opening on August 29, 2009. Its house brands include My Father, The Judge, Le Bijou 1922 and Flor de las Antillas. Cigar Aficionado named Flor de las Antillas its Cigar of the Year for 2012, Le Bijou 1922 for 2015 and The Judge Grand Robusto for 2024.</p>\n<p>The factory also makes cigars for other companies: Tatuaje, <a href=\"/node/latelier/\">L'Atelier</a>, <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a>, and Ashton's <a href=\"/node/laaromadecuba/\">La Aroma de Cuba</a> and San Cristóbal. The Garcías remain independent of the big cigar groups.</p>",
  "joseopadron": "<p><strong>José Orlando Padrón</strong> (1926–2017) founded <a href=\"/node/padron/\">Padrón Cigars</a> in Miami in 1964. He grew up in Cuba's tobacco country, lost his livelihood after the revolution and reached Miami in 1962 with almost nothing. The company's \"hammer story\" says that a friend, Raul Fernandez, gave him a small hammer. He used it for carpentry jobs while working as a gardener, and saved $600 to open a small cigar shop. The hammer is still part of the company's identity, and the 1964 Anniversary Series is named for the year Padrón began.</p>\n<p>In 1970 Padrón opened a factory in Estelí, Nicaragua, long before most U.S. brands made cigars there. The Nicaraguan operation is <a href=\"/node/tabacoscubanica/\">Tabacos Cubanica</a>. Padrón kept the business going through revolution, war and political risk in Nicaragua. He was known for long aging and a short list of blends rather than chasing trends.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/node/padron1926/\">Serie 1926</a> is named for the year he was born. Padrón died in Miami in December 2017, at 91. His son <a href=\"/node/jorgepadron/\">Jorge Padrón</a> leads the company now. It is still family-owned and still built around the rules he set: Nicaraguan tobacco, long aging and a small range of cigars.</p>",
  "ajfernandez": "<p><strong>Abdel J. \"A.J.\" Fernandez</strong> is a Cuban-born blender and manufacturer who built one of Estelí's largest independent operations. He was born in March 1979 in San Luis, Pinar del Río. His grandfather Andres ran a small Cuban factory and brand called San Lotano; his father Ismael worked in Cuban tobacco, then left in the mid-1990s to farm with his cousin <a href=\"/node/nestorplasencia/\">Nestor Plasencia</a> in Nicaragua. A.J. followed in <strong>2003</strong>, leaving Cuba on a family visit and staying. He started as a contract blender: catalog cigars such as <a href=\"/node/manowar/\">Man O' War</a> and <a href=\"/node/diesel/\">Diesel</a>, plus early work for <a href=\"/node/rockypatel/\">Rocky Patel</a> and <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa</a> lines including <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a>. He bought a farm in Estelí and named it San Lotano after the family marca.</p>\n<p>His own consumer brands came later. San Lotano returned as an A.J. line around <strong>2010</strong>. New World (<strong>2014</strong>), blended with his father Ismael, became the volume face of the house. Enclave, Last Call, Bellas Artes, and related limiteds followed. Production sits at <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a> in Estelí, with a second factory, <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a>, in Ocotal, opened in 2017 in the former San Rafael factory of Fidel Olivas. He grows leaf in Estelí, Condega, Jalapa, and other Nicaraguan regions. Who makes A.J. Fernandez cigars? He does, in his own factories, from a mix of estate and purchased tobacco.</p>\n<p>The other half of the business is blending for other companies. <a href=\"/node/foundation/\">Foundation</a>'s Tabernacle and Olmec are the boutique example. With <a href=\"/node/rafaelnodal/\">Rafael Nodal</a> and <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> he has blended Nicaraguan projects for <a href=\"/node/montecristonc/\">Montecristo (NC)</a>, <a href=\"/node/trinidadespiritu/\">Trinidad Espiritu</a>, <a href=\"/node/hupmannnc/\">H. Upmann (NC)</a>, <a href=\"/node/hoyonc/\">Hoyo de Monterrey (NC)</a>'s La Amistad, and Nicaraguan <a href=\"/node/agingroom/\">Aging Room</a>. Other clients on this graph include <a href=\"/node/rojas/\">Rojas</a>, and <a href=\"/node/lapalina/\">La Palina</a>. He is not STG, and he is not a logo on someone else's floor. House brands on one side, other people's cigars on the other, across his two factories.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/node/southerndraw/\">Southern Draw</a> is another long-running client: the Holt family's Kudzu has been made by A.J. Fernandez since 2014, followed by Rose of Sharon, Jacob's Ladder, and Cedrus.</p>",
  "erikespinosa": "<p><strong>Erik Espinosa</strong> founded <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a> and runs <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> in Estelí. In 2003 he and <a href=\"/node/eddieortega/\">Eddie Ortega</a> started <a href=\"/node/eobrands/\">EO Brands</a>, whose <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a> and <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a> were rolled by the García family at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a>. Around 2010 Rocky Patel took a 50% stake in EO and distributed it. An earlier version of this page dated EO to the \"mid-2000s\"; it was 2003.</p>\n<p>In July 2012 Espinosa and Ortega bought back Patel's stake and split the brands. Espinosa kept 601, Murciélago and Mi Barrio and sold them through his new Espinosa Premium Cigars. He moved production to La Zona and to Rocky Patel's <a href=\"/node/tavicusa/\">TaviCusa</a>. La Zona makes the Espinosa Habano, Crema and <a href=\"/node/laranja/\">Laranja</a> lines. 601 and Murciélago are now made by <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>.</p>\n<p>In 2022 Espinosa launched <a href=\"/node/knucklesandwich/\">Knuckle Sandwich</a> with <a href=\"/node/guyfieri/\">Guy Fieri</a>. Espinosa blended it and handles sales, but it is rolled at A.J. Fernández's <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory, not at La Zona as an earlier version of this page said. Espinosa Premium Cigars marked its 10th anniversary in 2022 with two cigars. He is an independent brand owner and factory operator, not part of STG.</p>",
  "guyfieri": "<p><strong>Guy Fieri</strong>, the restaurateur and Emmy-winning TV host, is the name behind <a href=\"/node/knucklesandwich/\">Knuckle Sandwich</a>, a cigar line made with <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a>'s <a href=\"/node/espinosa/\">Espinosa Premium Cigars</a>. The project took about 18 months. It was announced in January 2022, previewed in February 2022 at CHICA Miami during the South Beach Wine &amp; Food Festival alongside Fieri's Santo Tequila, and reached stores that month, a week ahead of schedule.</p>\n<p>Espinosa blended both cigars and handles sales and shipping. They are rolled at <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>'s <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal, Nicaragua, not at Espinosa's own <a href=\"/node/lazona/\">La Zona</a> in Estelí, as an earlier version of this page said. The Habano uses an Ecuadorian habano wrapper, a black foot ribbon and red boxes. The Maduro uses an Ecuadorian Sumatra wrapper, a red ribbon and black boxes. Both have Nicaraguan binder and filler.</p>\n<p>Each blend launched in three sizes: Corona Gorda R (5⅝ x 46), Robusto J (5 x 52) and Toro H (6 x 54). The letters after the names refer to people important in Fieri's life. Fieri owns the name; the cigars are made by Espinosa and A.J. Fernández.</p>",
  "eddieortega": "<p><strong>Eddie Ortega</strong> co-founded EO Brands (United Tobacco), the <a href=\"/node/eobrands/\">EO Brands</a> node on this map, with <a href=\"/node/erikespinosa/\">Erik Espinosa</a> in 2003. EO's brands included <a href=\"/node/sixzeroone/\">601</a>, <a href=\"/node/murcielago/\">Murciélago</a>, Cubao and Mi Barrio. Over the years it had distribution and manufacturing agreements with Miami Cigar &amp; Company, <a href=\"/node/rockypatel/\">Rocky Patel</a> and <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a>, where Don Pepín García's factory rolled 601 and Murciélago. An earlier version of this page tied EO's cigars to A.J. Fernández; we found no source for that.</p>\n<p>About 2010 Rocky Patel took a 50% stake in EO and handled its distribution. Ortega resigned in early 2012 and launched Ortega Cigar Company in Sunrise, Florida. His first line, Ortega Serie D, was made at My Father in Estelí. In July 2012 he and Espinosa bought back Patel's stake and split the brands. Espinosa took 601, Murciélago and Mi Barrio, and Ortega kept Cubao plus the dormant REO and Vibe marks.</p>\n<p>In 2013 Ortega released The Wild Bunch, a monthly limited series first announced as the \"Dirty Dozen.\" One release, Island Jim \"WaHoo,\" honors Pittsburgh retailer <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">Jim Robinson</a>. Ortega had given him the nickname \"Island Jim.\"</p>",
  "jorgepadron": "<p><strong>Jorge Padrón</strong> is president of <a href=\"/node/padron/\">Padrón Cigars</a> and the youngest son of founder <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a>, who started the company in Miami in 1964. Jorge spent his childhood summers at the factory. He earned a marketing degree from Florida State University and an MBA from the University of Miami before joining his father.</p>\n<p>Until the early 1990s Padrón was well known in Miami but had little presence nationally. Jorge pushed the company onto the trade-show circuit. In 1993 he, his brother <a href=\"/node/orlandopadron/\">Orlando</a> and family friend Willy Pujals took a home-built booth to the RTDA show in Dallas and came back with 12 new retailers. That began Padrón's national expansion. In 1994 the company released the <a href=\"/node/padron1964/\">1964 Anniversary Series</a> for its 30th anniversary, and it sold out immediately.</p>\n<p>Jorge has said that consistency is the company's marketing. Padrón makes what its tobacco stocks allow instead of producing to fill orders, and it sells without a traditional sales force. By 2011 it operated from 22 buildings in Nicaragua, with the factory at <a href=\"/node/tabacoscubanica/\">Tabacos Cubanica</a> in Estelí. José Orlando Padrón died in 2017 at 91, and Jorge continues to lead the company.</p>",
  "orlandopadron": "<p><strong>Orlando \"Orly\" Padrón</strong> is the oldest son of <a href=\"/node/joseopadron/\">José Orlando Padrón</a>, who founded <a href=\"/node/padron/\">Padrón Cigars</a> in Miami in 1964. He came from Cuba in 1966 at age nine and has worked for the company ever since. As a boy he was dropped off at the factory after school, folded the papers that wrapped Padrón bundles, and cleaned returned boxes for the company's early box-recycling program.</p>\n<p>His brother <a href=\"/node/jorgepadron/\">Jorge Padrón</a> is the company's president. In 2011 Orlando managed distribution and represented the family at Padrón events around the country. In 1993 he went with Jorge and family friend Willy Pujals to the RTDA trade show in Dallas, where a home-built booth signed 12 new retailers and began Padrón's national expansion. His children Jeffrey and Jessica also worked in the office, with Jessica running the company's social media.</p>\n<p>Padrón still sells without a traditional sales force. In 2011 its cigars were made in Nicaragua and Honduras, with the Nicaraguan factory at <a href=\"/node/tabacoscubanica/\">Tabacos Cubanica</a> in Estelí, then shipped to Miami in bundles for packing. Orlando's own favorite, he said, is the <a href=\"/node/padron1926/\">Serie 1926</a> Maduro No. 6 (4¾ x 50). He finds big ring gauges uncomfortable. The family's best-known line is the <a href=\"/node/padron1964/\">1964 Anniversary</a>, launched in 1994 for the company's 30th year.</p>",
  "nestorplasencia": "<p><strong>Nestor Plasencia Sr.</strong> leads the Plasencia family's tobacco and cigar business. Cigar Journal described it in 2013 as tobacco growing on \"perhaps the largest scale in Central America.\" His father, Sixto, farmed about 130 acres of tobacco in San Luis, Pinar del Río, until Castro's land reform took the farm on October 3, 1963, when Nestor was 14. In 1965 the family flew to Mexico, then to Honduras, and within a month moved to Estelí. Nestor says they were the first Cuban family to settle there.</p>\n<p>Nestor studied agriculture, married in 1969 and joined his father in the Jalapa fields in 1970. When Nicaragua's revolution approached they rented land in Jamastran, Honduras, for the 1978–79 season. The family's Jalapa farm was later confiscated. Blue mold and heavy borrowing left Nestor about $10 million in debt by the end of the 1980s. In 1985 he started rolling cigars with ten rollers. When Swisher closed its Tampa factory in 1990, he won the contract to roll 60 percent of its cigars, and he had cleared his debt by 1991.</p>\n<p>After 1990 Nicaragua's new government offered him about 370 acres in Estelí. The family now runs factories in both countries, including <a href=\"/node/plasenciaesteli/\">Plasencia Estelí</a> and <a href=\"/node/plasenciahonduras/\">El Paraíso</a>, and makes cigars for <a href=\"/node/rockypatel/\">Rocky Patel</a> and others. His son <a href=\"/node/nestorandres/\">Nestor Andrés</a> helped launch the <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a> brand. Nestor Sr. received Cigar Journal's Cigar Trophy for Lifetime Achievement in 2012.</p>",
  "nestorandres": "<p><strong>Nestor Andrés Plasencia</strong> is a fifth-generation tobacco grower and cigar maker. He is the son of <a href=\"/node/nestorplasencia/\">Nestor Plasencia Sr.</a>, and with his brothers Gustavo and José Luis he launched the family's own brand, <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia Cigars</a>. The line traces the family back to Don Eduardo Plasencia, who began growing tobacco in Cuba in 1865.</p>\n<p>The family had sold a cigar under its own name before. The first Plasencia brand came out in 1987, a mild Connecticut-shade cigar made in Honduras and sold through JR Cigar. This time the effort was much bigger. Alma Fuerte, a Nicaraguan puro, debuted in October 2016 as the first cigar of the new Plasencia 1865 push. The brand formally launched in 2017 with the Alma series, Reserva Original and Cosecha 146, and the family took on its own U.S. distribution.</p>\n<p>Cosecha 146 uses only tobacco from the 2011–2012 crop on the family's farms in Nicaragua and Honduras, and it was made at the family's Tabacos de Oriente factory in Honduras. This map also links the family to <a href=\"/node/plasenciaesteli/\">Plasencia Estelí</a>, and to <a href=\"/node/plasenciahonduras/\">El Paraíso</a>. By 2022 the brand was sold in more than 80 countries, while the family kept making cigars for other companies.</p>",
  "carlitofuente": "<p><strong>Carlos \"Carlito\" Fuente Jr.</strong> is president of <a href=\"/node/arturo/\">Arturo Fuente</a> and the public face of the family company. He was born in Tampa, where grandfather Arturo Fuente started the house in 1912. After the family's Nicaraguan factory burned in 1979, Carlito and his father, Carlos Fuente Sr., rebuilt in the Dominican Republic in 1980 at <a href=\"/node/tabafuente/\">Tabacalera A. Fuente</a> in Santiago. He revived the perfecto shape with the Hemingway series in the mid-1980s and launched Don Carlos in 1986 as a tribute to his father. Carlos Sr. later said Carlito blended the house's cigars for decades. Sr. died in 2016. Carlito has run the company since.</p>\n<p>His defining project is <a href=\"/node/opusx/\">Fuente Fuente OpusX</a>, released in November 1995: the first commercially successful Dominican puro with Cuban-seed wrapper grown at Château de la Fuente. Critics had said the Dominican Republic could not grow that wrapper. OpusX proved otherwise and became a collector benchmark. Fuente also rolls partner brands at the same factory, including <a href=\"/node/ashton/\">Ashton</a>'s Dominican lines and <a href=\"/node/jcnewman/\">J.C. Newman</a>'s <a href=\"/node/diamondcrown/\">Diamond Crown</a>. In 2023 he broke ground on Gran Fábrica de Tabacos La Bella y La Bestia in Estelí, a return to Nicaragua. Fuente is independent of STG and Altadis. Who makes OpusX? The Fuente family, in the Dominican Republic, under Carlito.</p>",
  "rakeshpatel": "<p><strong>Rakesh \"Rocky\" Patel</strong> is the founder of <a href=\"/node/rockypatel/\">Rocky Patel Premium Cigars</a>. He was born in India (New Delhi, raised in Bombay), moved to the United States in 1974, went to high school in Green Bay, Wisconsin, and practiced entertainment and product-liability law in Los Angeles. He smoked cigars on studio lots, joined the Grand Havana Room in Beverly Hills as a founding member, and in <strong>1995</strong> invested in Indian Tabac Cigar Co. with Phillip Zanghi III, a boom-era startup that used an Indian Motorcycle name. Early production was in Honduras. He later took manufacturing under closer control with <a href=\"/node/nestorplasencia/\">Nestor Plasencia</a> at <a href=\"/node/plasenciahonduras/\">El Paraíso</a>.</p>\n<p>He put his own name on the company in <strong>2003</strong>. Vintage and The Edge became catalog fixtures. Brother <a href=\"/node/nishpatel/\">Nish Patel</a> joined in 2004. Around 2008-2009 Rocky opened <a href=\"/node/tavicusa/\">TaviCusa</a> (Tabacalera Villa Cuba) in Estelí with Amilcar Perez-Castro, so the catalog could mix owned Nicaraguan rolling with the Honduras Plasencia partnership. Headquarters are in Florida (Naples / Bonita Springs). He does not work for STG or Altadis. Who is Rocky Patel? The founder. Who makes Rocky Patel cigars? Plasencia in Honduras and TaviCusa in Nicaragua, under his company.</p>",
  "nishpatel": "<p><strong>Nish Patel</strong> is the younger brother of <a href=\"/node/rakeshpatel/\">Rocky Patel</a> and executive vice president of <a href=\"/node/rockypatel/\">Rocky Patel Premium Cigars</a>. He was born in Nairobi, Kenya, in 1964 and moved with his family to Green Bay, Wisconsin, as a child. He graduated from the University of Wisconsin–Oshkosh, earned an MBA from UCLA, and worked as a commodities broker and then as vice president of sales at ASI Advance Systems in Green Bay.</p>\n<p>After years of Rocky asking him, Nish moved to Florida in 2004 and joined the company as executive vice president. The company calls him its \"Swiss Army Knife,\" a wearer of many hats. Today he spends much of his time on the road hosting events. His own lines are Xen by Nish Patel (2011), a box-pressed Connecticut-shade cigar, and Bold by Nish Patel (2013), a maduro.</p>\n<p>In 2014 Cigar Aficionado listed \"Zen by Nish Patel\" among the first cigars made at the family's Estelí factory, <a href=\"/node/tavicusa/\">TaviCusa</a>, and noted that Nish and cousin Nimish Desai had brands of their own under the company umbrella. That year Nish celebrated his 50th birthday at the brothers' Naples cigar lounge, Burn. Many other Rocky Patel cigars are made by the Plasencia family in Honduras, at <a href=\"/node/plasenciahonduras/\">El Paraíso</a>.</p>",
  "jonathandrew": "<p><strong>Jonathan Drew Sann</strong> founded <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> in New York in 1996 with his fraternity brother Marvin Samel. While at Brooklyn Law School he ran QC Cigar Company, a kiosk in the World Trade Center's Tower Two, and worked as a rep for brands such as Tabacalera Perdomo, then called Nick's Cigars. His first brand, La Vieja Habana, began at a small Manhattan factory and moved to Miami. Production then went to <a href=\"/node/perdomo/\">Perdomo</a>'s factory in Nicaragua.</p>\n<p>After Hurricane Mitch destroyed that production in 1998, Drew moved to Estelí with his last money. He opened a first factory with six people in a rented house and rarely left Nicaragua for the next six years. <a href=\"/node/acid/\">Acid</a> was the first line to come out of that factory. After years spread across 11 small and mid-size factories, Drew Estate consolidated into <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica Drew Estate</a>, which Drew says was fully built by September 2008. <a href=\"/node/undercrown/\">Undercrown</a> began there, and <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a> grew into a major brand there.</p>\n<p>In 2014 <a href=\"/node/swisher/\">Swisher International</a> bought Drew Estate, which kept its existing management. Drew remains Founder, President and Chief Innovation Officer, working alongside CEO Glenn Wolfson. He has said his son, JD2, will one day take his role. <a href=\"/node/stevesaka/\">Steve Saka</a> was Drew Estate's president, then CEO, from 2005 to 2013.</p>",
  "christianeiroa": "<p><strong>Christian Luis Eiroa</strong> is the Honduran-based cigar maker behind <a href=\"/node/cle/\">CLE Cigar Company</a>, named after his initials. He was president and long-time public face of <a href=\"/node/camacho/\">Camacho Cigars</a>, the <a href=\"/node/eiroafamily/\">Eiroa family</a> brand. Camacho was made at the family's Tabacos Ranchos Jamastran factory in Honduras and is best known for its Corojo cigars.</p>\n<p>In 2008 Davidoff (<a href=\"/node/oettinger/\">Oettinger Davidoff</a>) bought Camacho. Eiroa stayed on as president through the transition. In late 2011 he said on Kiss My Ash Radio that he intended to remain in the business. CLE was announced in May 2012. The first cigars, CLE Cuarenta (Habano-seed wrapper) and CLE Corojo, were rolled at Ranchos Jamastran, where Camacho had been made. They were timed for July 2012, Eiroa's 40th birthday.</p>\n<p>In 2013 Eiroa opened his own factory in Danlí in the former Cine Aladino movie theater. It is shown here as the <a href=\"/node/eiroadanli/\">CLE factory</a>, and its first cigars were EIROA and CLE Plus. The Aladino cigar brand came in 2015 from his father, Julio Eiroa, and is made from Jamastran Valley tobacco at Julio's Las Lomas factory. Camacho remains with Davidoff. Christian's own company is CLE.</p>",
  "maxfernandez": "<p><strong>Max Fernández</strong> is the son of Aganorsa owner Eduardo Fernández Pujals and runs the factory and blending side of <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa Leaf</a>. That is the Nicaraguan grower and manufacturer behind much of the 2000s and 2010s boutique leaf. He manages the Estelí factory long known as <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> (Tabacos Valle de Jalapa S.A.). He has said he grew up visiting fermentation barns and factories with his father, Arsenio and the company's experts.</p>\n<p>His father founded Aganorsa (Agrícola Ganadera Norteña S.A.) in 1997, buying land in Jalapa and Condega, and later bought Tropical Tobacco in Miami. The cigar business was sold as Casa Fernández until April 2018, when it was renamed Aganorsa Leaf. Eduardo Fernández has said the renaming was Max's and sales chief Terence Reilly's decision. \"AGANORSA tobacco has always been unique,\" Max said at the time. \"You can recognize it when you taste a cigar made by us.\"</p>\n<p>Max is also a blending partner for client brands. Warped's Futuro grew out of a blending project between him and <a href=\"/node/kylegellis/\">Kyle Gellis</a>, with a Corojo '99 wrapper over Criollo '98 and Corojo '99 filler. TABSA has rolled cigars for <a href=\"/node/illusione/\">Illusione</a>, <a href=\"/node/warped/\">Warped</a>, <a href=\"/node/hvc/\">HVC</a> and <a href=\"/node/foundation/\">Foundation</a>. HVC founder <a href=\"/node/reinierlorenzo/\">Reinier Lorenzo</a> got his start after a 2009 trip to Aganorsa's Nicaraguan fields with Max's father.</p>",
  "nickmelillo": "<p><strong>Nicholas Melillo</strong> founded <a href=\"/node/foundation/\">Foundation Cigar Company</a> in 2015. He was born in New Haven, Connecticut. In March 2003 he went to work for <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua, and stayed until 2014. He worked in production and tobacco sourcing at <a href=\"/node/lagranfabrica/\">La Gran Fábrica</a> during the <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a> years, and he finished there as executive vice president of international operations.</p>\n<p>Foundation's first cigar was El Güegüense, named for a Nicaraguan folk drama and usually translated as \"The Wise Man.\" It was a Nicaraguan puro made by <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa Leaf</a> at <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a>, and the corona made Cigar Aficionado's Top 25 for 2016. Charter Oak, a Foundation line built on Connecticut tobacco, is made by A.J. Fernández.</p>\n<p>In 2024 Foundation left Aganorsa and retired both El Güegüense and the original Wise Man Maduro. The replacements, The Wise Man Corojo and The Wise Man Maduro, are made at <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father Cigars</a> in Estelí with Pepín García's family. The same year Foundation renamed its Metapa line Aksum, made in Nicaragua by A.J. Fernández. Foundation's Tabernacle and Olmec are made at <a href=\"/node/ajfesteli/\">Tabacalera A.J. Fernández</a>.</p>\n<p>Foundation owns no factory. Melillo blends the cigars and has them made by these partners, using what he learned in more than a decade on Drew Estate's factory floor.</p>",
  "kylegellis": "<p><strong>Kyle Gellis</strong> is the founder of <a href=\"/node/warped/\">Warped Cigars</a>, a boutique brand he started in 2009 while he was a student at the University of Central Florida. Warped does not own a factory. Its cigars are made mainly at <a href=\"/node/eltitandebronze/\">El Titan de Bronze</a> in Miami's Little Havana and at <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa Leaf</a>'s <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> factory in Estelí.</p>\n<p>Gellis's ties to Aganorsa run deep. Futuro is his collaboration with <a href=\"/node/maxfernandez/\">Max Fernández</a>, using Corojo '99 and Criollo '98 and made at TABSA. Flor del Valle, including the 7 x 40 Sky Flower Lancero, is rolled there too. Cloud Hopper is Warped's value line, built on Nicaraguan Corojo. The original Corto, a short smoke, is made at Aganorsa.</p>\n<p>When Warped launched Corto Maduro, it went to <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a> (Nicaragua American Cigars S.A.) in Estelí. The first size, the X46, is a box-pressed 4½ x 46 with a Mexican San Andrés maduro wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and filler. NACSA also makes Gellis Family Cigars' Absolutos, a 100% Nicaraguan sun-grown line. Warped has also used <a href=\"/node/laisla/\">La iSLA</a> in the Dominican Republic, which makes its Nicotina line.</p>\n<p>That spread of factories is typical of Gellis's approach: pick the floor that suits the blend rather than build one. He is often grouped with <a href=\"/node/diongiolito/\">Dion Giolito</a> and <a href=\"/node/andrefarkas/\">Andre Farkas</a> among boutique owners who built on Aganorsa leaf.</p>",
  "fredvandermarliere": "<p><strong>Fred (Frederik) Vandermarliere</strong> is the CEO of J. Cortès, the Belgian family cigar company that has owned <a href=\"/node/oliva/\">Oliva Cigar Co.</a> since 2016. Oliva's cigars are made at its <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> factory in Estelí, Nicaragua.</p>\n<p>J. Cortès specialized in cheaper machine-made cigars. Oliva added a premium, handmade Nicaraguan catalog and a strong U.S. presence. Vandermarliere signed the deal, and halfwheel reported that J. Cortès's bid beat offers from <a href=\"/node/stg/\">Scandinavian Tobacco Group</a> and at least one other company.</p>\n<p>The deal closed on June 30, 2016 and took effect July 1. The combined company had annual revenue above $100 million and about 3,000 employees. Oliva's management stayed: José Oliva remained CEO, and Gilberto Oliva Jr. kept running the Nicaraguan growing operations. Both companies said little would change in how Oliva sells cigars in the U.S. or abroad.</p>\n<p>J. Cortès then invested in the factory. Tabolisa II opened in 2018 with room for about 25,000 cigars a day, along with a new box factory, and the rebuilt Tabolisa I reopened in December 2019 with capacity raised from 55,000 to 90,000 cigars a day. \"To let a diamond shine, you need to work it,\" Vandermarliere said. Oliva's lines include Serie G, Serie O, <a href=\"/node/seriev/\">Serie V</a> and Melanio, Cigar Aficionado's Cigar of the Year in 2014, plus Studio Tobac's <a href=\"/node/nub/\">NUB</a> and <a href=\"/node/cain/\">Cain</a>.</p>",
  "nickperdomo": "<p><strong>Nick Perdomo Jr.</strong> founded Nick's Cigar Co. in August 1992, rolling cigars in his garage in Hialeah, near Miami. At the time he was an air-traffic controller at Miami International Airport, a job he had started in the U.S. Navy, and he kept it while the cigar business grew. His grandfather Silvio Perdomo had worked at H. Upmann and Partagás in Cuba before he was imprisoned after the revolution.</p>\n<p>The company sold about 9,400 cigars in its first year. It moved to Ybor City and outgrew that, and in the mid-1990s it opened production in Estelí, Nicaragua, with Nick Sr. coming out of retirement to help. The company became <a href=\"/node/perdomo/\">Perdomo Cigars</a>. The headquarters stayed in the Miami area.</p>\n<p>Today Perdomo Jr. leads the family company from <a href=\"/node/perdomofactory/\">Tabacalera Perdomo</a>, a 14-acre complex in Estelí. Its main factory, nicknamed \"El Monstro,\" is often described as Nicaragua's second-largest, and Perdomo reports output of about 15 million cigars a year. Every Perdomo line is made there, from the Reserve 10th Anniversary Champagne, one of its biggest sellers, to Lot 23, named for a farm plot whose leaf is aged at least five years.</p>\n<p>Perdomo is one of Estelí's large family-owned companies, alongside the Olivas at <a href=\"/node/tabolisa/\">Tabolisa</a> and the <a href=\"/node/plasencia/\">Plasencia</a> family. Like them, it ferments and ages its tobacco on its own campus, in multi-story warehouses.</p>",
  "alejandromartinez": "<p><strong>Dr. Alejandro Martínez Cuenca</strong> is the Nicaraguan economist and politician who owns <a href=\"/node/joya/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a>, the country's oldest cigar brand. He first visited the Estelí factory in 1979, when he was minister of trade and the company had been taken into public hands after Somoza fled. After the Sandinistas lost the 1990 election, the factory was loaned to its workers. That arrangement collapsed in 1992, and during the embargo years Joya was being made in Honduras by <a href=\"/node/nestorplasencia/\">Nestor Plasencia</a>.</p>\n<p>Martínez Cuenca bought the company in 1994 for $600,000, when only two or three people were still making cigars there. He rehired many of the factory's best-known rollers. In 1998 he bought the U.S. rights to the Joya de Nicaragua trademark from Altadis. Antaño 1970 came out in 2000.</p>\n<p>His son Juan Ignacio Martínez became executive president in 2013. The <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya factory</a> also makes cigars for other brands, including <a href=\"/node/dunbarton/\">Dunbarton</a>'s Sobremesa, <a href=\"/node/fratello/\">Fratello</a> and the first <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a> cigars. Drew Estate distributed Joya in the U.S. for about 17 years, until the deal ended on January 1, 2026. Joya then formed a new U.S. distributor, SAG, with Quesada Cigars.</p>",
  "stevesaka": "<p><strong>Steve Saka</strong> founded <a href=\"/node/dunbarton/\">Dunbarton Tobacco &amp; Trust</a> in 2015, a family-held company that is his own blender brand. Before that he worked from 2000 as an executive consultant to Lew Rothman, then owner of JR Cigar, for four years. He then served as president and later CEO of <a href=\"/node/drewestate/\">Drew Estate</a> from 2005 through 2013, the years when <a href=\"/node/ligaprivada/\">Liga Privada</a> became the company's prestige line next to <a href=\"/node/acid/\">Acid</a>.</p>\n<p>Dunbarton's first cigar was <a href=\"/node/sobremesa/\">Sobremesa</a>, made at <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a> in Estelí with tobacco, packaging and bands that Saka supplied. Mi Querida, introduced at the 2016 IPCPR show, uses a Connecticut broadleaf wrapper and is made at <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a>. Sin Compromiso is rolled at Joya by the factory's top torcedores. Muestra de Saka is a limited line of one-off blends and sizes. Dunbarton says all its cigars come from those two factories, Joya de Nicaragua and Nicaragua American Cigars S.A., with dedicated teams at both.</p>\n<p>Dunbarton also grows some of its own tobacco. It is the only company halfwheel's consensus has named Company of the Year four years in a row. Saka's former Drew Estate colleagues include founder <a href=\"/node/jonathandrew/\">Jonathan Drew</a> and <a href=\"/node/nickmelillo/\">Nick Melillo</a>, who later founded Foundation Cigar Co.</p>",
  "diongiolito": "<p><strong>Dion Giolito</strong> is the founder of <a href=\"/node/illusione/\">Illusione Cigars</a>, which are made mainly at Aganorsa Leaf's <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> factory in Estelí, Nicaragua. He owns no factory. He moved to Reno, Nevada, in 1988, worked at a Tinder Box store, and opened his own shop, Fumare, in 2004.</p>\n<p>A Davidoff-sponsored trip to the Dominican Republic that year, where he learned from Hendrik \"Henke\" Kelner, pushed him toward the production side. <a href=\"/node/petejohnson/\">Pete Johnson</a> of <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a> introduced him to Eduardo Fernández of Tropical Tobacco, later <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa Leaf</a>. In 2005 Giolito contracted Tropical to make his house brand, built around Corojo-seed leaf from Aganorsa's San José farm and rolled at <a href=\"/node/raicescubanas/\">Raíces Cubanas</a> in Honduras.</p>\n<p>Illusione debuted in 2006 as Fumare's in-house cigar. The name and lines such as 88, the year he moved back to Reno, play on conspiracy theories. By 2007 the nine-cigar lineup was drawing top ratings.</p>\n<p>From 2011 Giolito moved most production to TABSA, where the tobacco is stored. Epernay stayed at Raíces. Illusione later added Original Documents Habano (2023), made at A.J. Fernández's <a href=\"/node/sanlotano/\">San Lotano</a> factory in Ocotal. In 2017 Giolito bought OneOff and relaunched it as a puro made at TABSA. Illusione sits alongside <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje</a> and <a href=\"/node/warped/\">Warped</a> on the same Aganorsa leaf.</p>",
  "andrefarkas": "<p><strong>Andre Farkas</strong> founded <a href=\"/node/viaje/\">Viaje Cigars</a> around 2007–2008. It is a small-batch boutique. In 2019 its only regular-production line was Viaje Exclusivo, and everything else came out in limited batches, and its best-known line is the Zombie series, first released in 2011. Farkas has never owned a factory. Viaje has worked with factories in at least four countries.</p>\n<p>The first Viaje cigars were made at <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a> in 2007. Farkas left in 2008, after Drew Estate took over Joya's distribution and he felt he was in the way. He then worked with Eduardo Fernández's Casa Fernández (now <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa Leaf</a>), whose cigars were made at <a href=\"/node/raicescubanas/\">Raíces Cubanas</a> in Danlí, Honduras. From 2009 Raíces was Viaje's only factory until Casa Fernández Miami and <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a> in Estelí joined the rotation.</p>\n<p>From 2015 Viaje added Dominican factories, including <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR Cigars</a>, the first Viaje cigars made without Aganorsa tobacco. By 2019 Farkas said most releases were back at TABSA. Viaje has also made cigars at <a href=\"/node/laisla/\">Tabacalera La Isla</a>, and the Craft Series release Roots went back to Joya de Nicaragua. Viaje sits alongside <a href=\"/node/illusione/\">Illusione</a> and <a href=\"/node/warped/\">Warped</a> among the boutiques built on Aganorsa leaf.</p>",
  "reinierlorenzo": "<p><strong>Reinier Lorenzo</strong> founded <a href=\"/node/hvc/\">HVC Cigars</a> in 2011. HVC stands for Havana City, a nod to the city where he grew up. He was born in Camagüey, studied veterinary medicine in Cuba and left the island in 2008, at 23. He spent the next seven years working for a genetics company in Madison, Wisconsin, running HVC part-time until he went full-time in 2015. A 2009 trip to Nicaragua with <a href=\"/node/aganorsa/\">Aganorsa</a> owner Eduardo Fernández set his cigar career in motion.</p>\n<p>For its first decade HVC was a brand without a factory. Its cigars were made under contract at <a href=\"/node/tabsa/\">TABSA</a>, Aganorsa's Estelí factory, and later also at <a href=\"/node/joyafactory/\">Joya de Nicaragua</a>. The annual Black Friday release shows how that worked: the 2021 edition, a 4⅜ x 54 short toro with a Connecticut broadleaf wrapper, was rolled at TABSA in a run of 600 boxes of 50.</p>\n<p>In early 2022 Lorenzo opened his own factory in Estelí, Fábrica de Tabacos HVC S.A. de Reinier Lorenzo, now mapped as the <a href=\"/node/hvcfactory/\">HVC factory</a>. Its first line was <strong>Selección No. 1</strong>, a Mexican San Andrés wrapper over Nicaraguan binder and Estelí and Jalapa fillers, which debuted at the 2022 PCA show. At launch it was the only line made there, by five pairs of bunchers and rollers. \"I never forget where I came from, Havana City,\" Lorenzo told halfwheel, \"and I don't want, later in life, to regret not opening my own cigar factory.\"</p>",
  "rickrodriguez": "<p><strong>Rick Rodriguez</strong> is a Tampa blender and brand owner whose family story starts in Cuban rolling rooms. His grandparents, Emanuel and Olga Rodriguez, were Havana rollers who settled in West Tampa in the 1950s. Rick himself entered the trade in <strong>2000</strong> as a Florida salesman for <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a>, recruited by a friend from Lamaze class. In 2005 Edgar Cullman Sr. put him into General's blender program under Benji Menendez, Daniel Núñez, and <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a>. After years with Menendez he joined the La Gloria Cubana blending team in 2008, when Perez-Carrillo left to start what became <a href=\"/node/casacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a>.</p>\n<p>When <a href=\"/node/stg/\">STG</a> bought <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>, Rodriguez became CAO's master blender and brand ambassador from <strong>2010</strong> until he left in April <strong>2022</strong>. He was the public face of lines such as Brazilia, Flathead, and other CAO catalog cigars, and he has said he expected to stay there for life. Then his daughter Sara wanted into the business. He retired from General and, with Sara and fellow General alumnus Gus Martinez (president), launched <a href=\"/node/westtampa/\">West Tampa Tobacco Co.</a> in 2022. White, Black, Red, and the Attic Series tell Tampa and family stories on the boxes, not just blend codes.</p>\n<p>Who makes West Tampa cigars? Core production is at <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a> in Estelí, with some work also recorded at <a href=\"/node/nacsa/\">NACSA</a>. Rodriguez blends and owns. He does not run an STG factory. CAO remains on this map as the corporate chapter of his career. West Tampa is the independent one. If you are asking who Rick Rodriguez is, that split is the answer: longtime CAO blender, then family boutique in the old cigar capital's name.</p>",
  "chantelleavitt": "<p><strong>Chantel Leavitt</strong> is the founder and CEO of <a href=\"/node/belladamacigars/\">Bella Dama Cigars</a>, a boutique brand in St. Petersburg, Florida, built around the world of poker. Its lines carry card-table names such as The King, The Queen and Full House. She started with a U.S. manufacturer and a friend who helped with her first three blends.</p>\n<p>The biggest change came in 2024. After sampling with several factories in 2023, Leavitt announced on August 12, 2024 that <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraaragon/\">Tabacalera Aragón</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua, would be the sole producer of all Bella Dama cigars. The brand relaunched on September 1, 2024. It kept its six existing cigars but upgraded their wrappers, binders and fillers, and it added Dealers Select, a lower-priced range of three cigars. Leavitt also refreshed the trademarked logo and said the brand would expand into brick-and-mortar shops.</p>\n<p>Leavitt owns a brand, not a factory, which is the usual model for small American labels. <a href=\"/node/definition/\">Definition</a> works the same way, and so do the house brands that <a href=\"/node/garmendia/\">Garmendia</a> rolls for shops, all leaning on small Estelí workshops. Aragón, run by Roniel and Roidel Aragón, also made <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a> cigars from 2018 to 2024, so Bella Dama arrived at a factory that already had experience with independent brands.</p>",
  "karenbergerperson": "<p><strong>Karen Berger</strong> owns and leads <a href=\"/node/karenberger/\">Karen Berger Cigars</a> and the <a href=\"/node/tabacaleraesteli/\">Tabacalera Estelí</a> factory. She was born in Estelí, Nicaragua, and joined the industry at 18 as a roller at the factory she now owns, working to pay for her business administration degree. There she met its owner, Enrique \"Don Kiki\" Berger, and they were married for 17 years.</p>\n<p>Don Kiki's father, Max Berger, had fled Poland for Cuba and set up a factory in Pinar del Río. Kiki moved to Nicaragua during the 1990s cigar boom, bought farmland and founded the factory, then launched Don Kiki Red, Green, White and Brown in 1995. He was diagnosed with chronic kidney failure in 2001. In 2006, after five years of dialysis, Karen donated a kidney to him. He died in Miami on September 23, 2014.</p>\n<p>Karen kept the business going and added her own K by Karen Berger line in 2016. It is a box-pressed Nicaraguan puro, and the \"K\" honors both her and Kiki. In 2022 she worked with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a> on a 25th-anniversary cigar. She also runs the Don Kiki Cigars Superstore in Daytona Beach, Florida. Later she moved the factory into a former three-story hotel in central Estelí so its 28 artisans could work close to home. Her farm grows mainly Habano Criollo on about 62 acres, and the factory makes about half a million cigars a year.</p>",
  "billpaley": "<p>William \"Bill\" Paley is the great-grandson of Samuel Paley, who founded Chicago's Congress Cigar Factory in <strong>1896</strong> and named the original <strong>La Palina</strong> marca after his wife, Goldie Drell Paley. Bill revived the family brand in <strong>2010</strong> on a New York City rooftop, framing it as a personal signature luxury cigar in the spirit of his grandfather's dedication to excellence and his parents' meticulous style. An early Graycliff partnership in the Bahamas (near the Paley family's Lyford Cay estate) proved short-lived, pushing La Palina toward the multi-country negociant model it uses today.</p>\n<p>La Palina does not own a factory, but it does own its tobacco, brands, and finished cigars. Bill brought aboard industry veterans <strong>Samuel \"Sammy\" Phillips</strong> and <strong>Clay Roberts</strong> to shape the modern company; he remains chairman and principal while they steward day-to-day operations. The portfolio's apex <strong>Goldie</strong> cigars roll at <a href=\"/node/eltitandebronze/\">El Titan de Bronze</a> in Miami, with additional production in Honduras, Nicaragua through <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernandez</a>, and the Dominican Republic including <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR Cigars</a>. Bill Paley connects a Gilded Age Chicago cigarmaking dynasty, and the CBS broadcasting fortune it helped seed, to one of today's most distinctive American heritage revivals.</p>",
  "noelrojas": "<p><strong>Noel Rojas</strong> is a Cuban-American cigar maker, founder of <a href=\"/node/rojas/\">Rojas Cigars</a>, and the self-styled \"King of Small Gauge.\" He first drew attention at the 2013 IPCPR trade show with <a href=\"/node/guayacan/\">Guayacan</a>. At the time he already owned and ran his own Estelí factory, Tabacalera Aromas de Jalapa, which was unusual for a newcomer.</p>\n<p>After the partners at Aromas de Jalapa split in late 2014, Rojas and Renegade Cigars owner Brandon Hayes opened <a href=\"/node/neworder/\">New Order of the Ages</a> (NOA) on January 1, 2016. NOA made Rojas's own lines and boutique brands such as <a href=\"/node/jassumkral/\">Jas Sum Kral</a>, but it was reported closed by mid-2018, when Rojas was working from a small factory in Texas.</p>\n<p>In 2021 he returned to Estelí and turned an old movie theater into the <a href=\"/node/rojasfactory/\">Rojas Cigar Factory</a>, about 7,000 square feet with room for some 40 pairs of rollers. He has since bought farms in Condega and Jalapa, and he says 100 percent of the filler and binder in every cigar he makes comes from them. The factory also makes <a href=\"/node/stolenthrone/\">Stolen Throne</a>, and the two brands show side by side at PCA. Recent Rojas releases include the KSG small-gauge series, the Street Tacos line with its annual Cinco de Mayo limited editions, and Quiet Morning (2026).</p>",
  "littogomez": "<p><strong>Litto Gomez</strong> is the co-founder of <a href=\"/node/lfd/\">La Flor Dominicana</a>. Before cigars he ran a jewelry business in Miami. According to Cigar Aficionado, he left it after a violent robbery. In 1994 he and Inés Lorenzo-Gomez started making cigars in the Dominican Republic under the name Los Libertadores. After taking over the operation in 1996, they renamed it La Flor Dominicana.</p>\n<p>Gomez decided to grow his own tobacco. In 1997 he bought the La Canela farm, where he went after strong ligero leaf. That became the house style. The Double Ligero line is the signature of LFD's power, and in 2003 the company introduced the Chisel, a wedge-shaped figurado that became its best-known shape. The Andalusian Bull was Cigar Aficionado's Cigar of the Year for 2016, and in 2024 the company marked 30 years with its LFD 30 Years release.</p>\n<p>Gomez runs <a href=\"/node/lfdfactory/\">Tabacalera La Flor</a> in Tamboril, where LFD's cigars are rolled. La Flor Dominicana is a member of ProCigar, the Dominican manufacturers' association, alongside <a href=\"/node/pdr/\">PDR</a> and <a href=\"/node/laaurora/\">La Aurora</a>.</p>\n<p>Gomez came to cigars as an outsider, without a family factory behind him, unlike <a href=\"/node/jochyblanco/\">Jochy Blanco</a> at <a href=\"/node/lapalma/\">Tabacalera Palma</a>, whose father founded that factory in 1925. He built a company that grows much of its own leaf on farms such as La Canela and rolls it in its own factory. That is why Cigar Nexus groups LFD with the family-owned Dominican houses rather than with brands that rely on contract factories.</p>",
  "jimrobinson": "<p><strong>\"Island\" Jim Robinson</strong> is a Pittsburgh cigar retailer and brand owner, known for his Leaf &amp; Bean shop in the city's Strip District. <a href=\"/node/eddieortega/\">Eddie Ortega</a> gave him the nickname \"Island Jim,\" a nod to his tropical shirts and his years living in Key West. Before cigars, Robinson worked for Marriott, traveling for weeks at a time to do training and quality control at hotels around the country. He gave that up and moved back to Pittsburgh.</p>\n<p>Robinson created <a href=\"/node/leafbyoscar/\">Leaf by Oscar</a> as a house cigar for his shop. halfwheel reported in 2016 that it had reached nearly 1,000 retailers. It is made by <a href=\"/node/oscarvalladares/\">Oscar Valladares</a> in Danlí, Honduras, and halfwheel credits Robinson's brands with helping make that factory possible. The <a href=\"/node/islandjim/\">Island Jim</a> line, including the No. 2, began as Leaf &amp; Bean's private label and comes from the same factory.</p>\n<p>With <a href=\"/node/nicholassyris/\">Nicholas Syris</a> of LH Premium he created <a href=\"/node/nickandjim/\">Nick &amp; Jim</a>. He appeared in Ortega's Wild Bunch series; its third cigar was named for \"wahoo,\" the word he uses for everything from hello to a celebration. He is also known for his yellow sunglasses. Lee Marsh has said Robinson was one of the people who introduced him to Noel Rojas, the start of <a href=\"/node/stolenthrone/\">Stolen Throne</a>.</p>",
  "nicholassyris": "<p>Nicholas Syris is a Greek-American master blender and founder of <a href=\"/node/lhpremium/\">LH Premium Cigars</a> (Lavida Habana). Born in Neapolis, Kozani, in the Macedonian region of Greece, he grew up in a tobacco-farming family before emigrating to the United States as a child, sorting leaves on summer visits back to Greece and later discovering premium cigars after college. A successful audio-video entrepreneur whose cigar friendships opened doors to Saudi royal clientele, Syris built Lavida Habana private lounges abroad with custom Cuban rolls for lounge clientele, while his non-Cuban <a href=\"/node/lhpremium/\">LH</a> blends are rolled at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>.</p>\n<p>Core lines, Claro, Colorado, and Maduro, share Ecuadorian wrappers from the same farm over Nicaraguan, Peruvian, Dominican, Honduran, and Brazilian filler. The Nick line includes the fuller-bodied Nikos extension, but Nicolas, a San Andrés-wrapped cigar, is by far his most full-bodied Nick release. Syris met Pittsburgh retailer <a href=\"/node/jimrobinson/\">\"Island Jim\" Robinson</a> in Cuba, blended a house cigar for Leaf &amp; Bean, and released it nationally as <a href=\"/node/nickandjim/\">Nick &amp; Jim</a> after Lavida Isla naming hurdles. For the map, Syris anchors a Costa Rican boutique thread, international lounge culture meeting verifiable Central American production at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>.</p>",
  "nelsonalfonso": "<p><strong>Nelson Alfonso Egüed</strong> founded <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco S.A.</a>, the Costa Rican company behind <a href=\"/node/atabey/\">Atabey</a>, <a href=\"/node/byron/\">Byron</a>, Bandolero and Alfonso. He started out as a graphic artist. He designed for Habanos S.A. brands such as Cohiba, Montecristo, Partagás and Romeo y Julieta, published the multimedia work \"Puros Habanos,\" and in 2010 created the branding for the Cohiba Behike.</p>\n<p>Alfonso launched Selected Tobacco at the 2012 IPCPR trade show in Orlando, with Atabey and Bandolero plus a revived Byron. Byron was founded in 1850 and named for the poet Lord Byron. Alfonso's family made it in the 19th century and later sold it, and he brought it back. For about a year the cigars went only to the 33 shops in United Cigar Retailers. Wider U.S. distribution opened in July 2014.</p>\n<p>The cigars are rolled at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a> in Santiago de Puriscal. Alfonso does not publish blends: \"I leave the tobaccos blends to the imagination of the smoker.\" After rolling, cigars go to Madrid for three years of aging in humidors lined with five types of cedar. In 2025 the company marked Byron's 175th anniversary. <a href=\"/node/elseptimo/\">El Septimo</a>, owned by <a href=\"/node/zayayounan/\">Zaya Younan</a>, is a separate Costa Rican luxury brand.</p>",
  "ernestoperezcarrillo": "<p><strong>Ernesto Perez-Carrillo Jr.</strong> is the blender who made non-Cuban <a href=\"/node/lagloriacubana/\">La Gloria Cubana</a> a 1990s phenomenon, then started over as <a href=\"/node/casacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a> / E.P. Carrillo. He was born in Pinar del Río in 1951. His father, Ernesto Sr., bought Havana's El Credito factory in 1948, served in the Cuban Senate, and fled to Miami after the Revolution. Ernesto Jr. spent years as a jazz drummer, including a stretch in New York, then came back to Little Havana. When his father died in 1980 he took over El Credito. A 90-point rating in an early issue of <em>Cigar Aficionado</em> (around 1992) turned La Gloria Cubana Wavell into the boutique cigar of the boom. He later opened Dominican capacity to keep up.</p>\n<p>In <strong>1999</strong> the family sold El Credito and La Gloria Cubana to Swedish Match, which folded the marca into <a href=\"/node/generalcigar/\">General Cigar</a> (now under <a href=\"/node/stg/\">STG</a>). Perez-Carrillo stayed about ten years as blender and ambassador. General also credited him, with Daniel Núñez, Benji Menendez, and Estelo Padrón, on the 2005 relaunch of non-Cuban <a href=\"/node/bolivar_nc/\">Bolivar</a>. In that stretch he developed La Gloria Cubana Serie R in large ring gauges, a hit that helped make 60-ring cigars normal. He left in March <strong>2009</strong>. His children, Lissette and Ernesto III, asked him to build a family company again. They launched E.P. Carrillo that year. The Santiago factory, leased in April 2009 as Tabacalera La Alianza, was renamed <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Casa Carrillo</a> in 2024. La Gloria Cubana the brand still belongs to General/Forged, not to him, though in 2025 he blended La Gloria Cubana Los Gloriosos at his own factory for that corporate line.</p>\n<p>House cigars from that floor include Pledge, Encore, La Historia, INCH, and related lines. <em>Cigar Aficionado</em> named Encore Majestic Cigar of the Year for 2018 and Pledge Prequel Cigar of the Year for 2020. The Santiago factory also takes contract work (including some <a href=\"/node/crownedheads/\">Crowned Heads</a>, Gurkha, Ozgener and Caldwell projects on this graph). Perez-Carrillo is a blender-operator, not an STG employee. If you are asking who he is: Miami La Gloria, then General, then his own Dominican house.</p>",
  "estebandisla": "<p><strong>Esteban Disla</strong> is a Nicaraguan cigar maker and blender and the co-founder of <a href=\"/node/domain/\">Domain Cigars</a>. Most of his career was spent inside other people's factories. He first worked at Latin Cigars S.A., the Estelí factory now known as <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>. There, according to Domain's launch release, he created blends for <a href=\"/node/cao/\">CAO</a>, Toraño, <a href=\"/node/alecbradley/\">Alec Bradley</a> and some 30 other companies.</p>\n<p>He then spent just over ten years at <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Fábrica de Tabacos Nica Sueño</a>, the Estelí factory that is home to <a href=\"/node/romacraft/\">RoMa Craft Tobac</a>. That gave him a long run at one of the most respected boutique factories in Nicaragua before he set out on his own.</p>\n<p>In 2024 Disla and <a href=\"/node/daniellance/\">Daniel Lance</a>, a 17-year industry veteran and co-founder of the BTX cutter maker Lujo Concepts, launched Domain. Its cigars are made at <a href=\"/node/familiadisla/\">Tabacalera Familia Disla S.A.</a> in Estelí. The first lines were Neutron, with a Mexican San Andrés wrapper and Condega and Pueblo Nuevo fillers, and Negentropy, with an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper and Condega filler. Both came in nine sizes. Entropy, with U.S.-grown Connecticut broadleaf, came later. Domain credits the cigars to the family's Despanic fermentation process, which groups tobacco primings so oils and flavor compounds pass between leaves.</p>",
  "daniellance": "<p><strong>Daniel Lance</strong> is a co-founder of <a href=\"/node/domain/\">Domain Cigars</a>, which is made at <a href=\"/node/familiadisla/\">Tabacalera Familia Disla S.A.</a> in Estelí, Nicaragua, the factory he and blender <a href=\"/node/estebandisla/\">Esteban Disla</a> formed in 2024.</p>\n<p>He spent 17 years in the cigar business before Domain, starting in retail at Outlaw Cigar Company and later Fidel's Cigar Shop, and then advising cigar shops on difficult markets and on mergers and acquisitions. He also co-founded Lujo Concepts, the accessories company that makes the BTX cutter. Outside cigars, he has advised government agencies on modernizing critical infrastructure and worked with startups in industrial cybersecurity.</p>\n<p>In March 2024 Lance and Disla announced Domain and Tabacalera Familia Disla together, with invitations to 100 retailers. Disla brings the blending side. He worked at Latin Cigars, the factory now called <a href=\"/node/stgesteli/\">STG Estelí</a>, and spent just over 10 years at <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Nica Sueño</a>, the home of RoMa Craft, which he built with <a href=\"/node/skipmartin/\">Skip Martin</a>.</p>\n<p>Domain launched with two lines, Neutron and Negentropy, in nine sizes, with shipping from April 2024. Neutron uses a Mexican San Andrés wrapper. Negentropy uses an Ecuadorian Habano wrapper. According to the company, Tabacalera Familia Disla went from holding no tobacco to producing 300,000 pounds a year in five years.</p>",
  "petejohnson": "<p><strong>Pete Johnson</strong> is the U.S. brand owner behind <a href=\"/node/tatuaje/\">Tatuaje</a> (2003) and <a href=\"/node/latelier/\">L'Atelier</a> (2012). He came out of Los Angeles cigar retail, including the Grand Havana Room, and named Tatuaje (Spanish for tattoo) for his ink. In 2003 he met <a href=\"/node/pepin/\">Pepín García</a> in California; Pepín rolled the first Tatuajes at <a href=\"/node/elreyhabanos/\">El Rey de los Habanos</a> in Miami. Tatuaje was Pepín's first private-label client. That pairing, Pete's marca and García Cuban-style construction, helped start the 2000s boutique wave.</p>\n<p>Johnson does not own a factory. Who makes his cigars? The García family. When they built <a href=\"/node/myfather/\">My Father</a> in Estelí, Tatuaje moved with them. L'Atelier Imports followed in 2012 as a second company (with brother K.C. and partners), using Sancti Spíritus hybrid leaf and wine-world names, still rolled with the Garcías. Tatuaje is the tattoo-shop brand. L'Atelier is the workshop brand. Neither is an STG or Altadis name.</p>\n<p>Pete is a brand owner, not a grower or factory operator. Family-lore talk sometimes links him to Janny García and <a href=\"/node/sancristobal/\">San Cristóbal</a>. The commercial fact is simpler: García manufacturing, Johnson marcas. Tatuaje first, then L'Atelier, then El Rey and My Father for where the cigars are made.</p>",
  "skipmartin": "<p><strong>Skip Martin</strong> is the \"Ma\" in <a href=\"/node/romacraft/\">RoMa Craft Tobac</a> and co-founder, with <a href=\"/node/estebandisla/\">Esteban Disla</a>, of the <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">NicaSueño</a> factory in Estelí. He became a cigar smoker during the 1990s boom. In 2006 he was consulting for a friend opening a tobacco shop in Galveston, Texas, and three days in he bought his friend out. Hurricane Ike destroyed the shop, Hava Cigar, in September 2008.</p>\n<p>While rebuilding, Martin set out to make a private-label cigar to sell to his customers by mail order. He teamed up with <a href=\"/node/michaelrosales/\">Michael Rosales</a> of Costa Rican Imports. Their Costa Rican blends didn't work out, so they moved to Estelí and worked with Disla on what became CroMagnon. In 2011 they built NicaSueño, a 2,400-square-foot factory. In January 2012 Martin's Galveston Bay Tobacco merged with Rosales's company to form RoMa Craft. \"Michael Rosales and I built RoMa Craft Tobac. Esteban Disla and I built Nica Sueno,\" Martin has said.</p>\n<p>For his 50th birthday in July 2022, Martin made Quinquagenario with <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a> at <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Tabacalera La Alianza</a>. It was a single 5 x 50 robusto limited to 50,000 cigars, and the first RoMa cigar made outside NicaSueño.</p>",
  "michaelrosales": "<p><strong>Michael Rosales</strong> is the \"Ro\" in <a href=\"/node/romacraft/\">RoMa Craft Tobac</a>, which he co-founded with <a href=\"/node/skipmartin/\">Skip Martin</a> (\"Ma\"). Before RoMa, Rosales ran Costa Rican Imports, LLC. Its Adrians Costa Rica brand launched in 2007, and by 2012 more than 65 tobacconists carried it after years of Rosales selling on the road.</p>\n<p>RoMa Craft was announced in Austin, Texas, in January 2012. It merged Costa Rican Imports with Martin's Galveston Bay Tobacco, LLC, combining the Adrians, CroMagnon, CroMagnon Aquitaine, Intemperance, Thirty and Hava Cigar brands. It also combined manufacturing in Estelí, Nicaragua, and San José, Costa Rica. In 2011 the two had already built <a href=\"/node/nicasueno/\">Fábrica de Tabacos NicaSueño</a>, a 2,400-square-foot factory in Estelí. CroMagnon, their first joint cigar from it, made more than a dozen best-of-2011 lists. The partners started with direct-to-consumer sales through Hava Cigar Online, then planned to expand into brick-and-mortar shops.</p>\n<p>Quinquagenario is a three-part series for the partners' 50th birthdays. Each is a 5 x 50 robusto limited to 50,000 cigars and made at a factory other than NicaSueño. Martin's edition, planned for his 50th birthday in July 2022, was developed with <a href=\"/node/ernestoperezcarrillo/\">Ernesto Perez-Carrillo</a>'s <a href=\"/node/lacarrillo/\">Tabacalera La Alianza</a>, which made it. The next two editions mark the 50th birthdays of NicaSueño's <a href=\"/node/estebandisla/\">Esteban Disla</a> (2024) and Rosales (2026). In 2021 the factories for those two were still undecided.</p>",
  "rafaelnodal": "<p><strong>Rafael Nodal</strong> is the Cuban-born owner of <a href=\"/node/boutiqueblends/\">Boutique Blends</a>, the Miami company behind <a href=\"/node/agingroom/\">Aging Room</a>, and head of product capability at Tabacalera USA, the parent of <a href=\"/node/altadis/\">Altadis U.S.A.</a> He blends both his own brands and Altadis releases, which are made at partner factories in the Dominican Republic and Nicaragua.</p>\n<p>Nodal led the Miami group from 2002, when he, his wife Alina and partner Hank Bischoff took over Habana Cuba Cigar Co., which became Oliveros. The company restructured as Boutique Blends in 2012. Its Aging Room, La Bohème, SWAG and Oliveros lines leaned on <a href=\"/node/jochyblanco/\">Jochy Blanco</a>'s Tabacalera Palma in the Dominican Republic.</p>\n<p>In 2015 Boutique Blends and Altadis made Romeo by Romeo y Julieta Aging Room Small Batch F25 at Palma. In May 2017 Altadis took over Boutique Blends' sales and distribution, and Nodal joined Altadis to work on product quality and innovation. Trinidad Santiago, announced that August, was his first Altadis project and the company's first regular release from Palma. In October 2017 Tabacalera USA named him head of product capability, covering Altadis, JR Cigar and Casa de Montecristo and reporting to Javier Estades.</p>\n<p>Inside Altadis he is the public blender of Nicaraguan projects with <a href=\"/node/ajfernandez/\">A.J. Fernández</a>, including <a href=\"/node/trinidadespiritu/\">Trinidad Espiritu</a>, Montecristo Nicaragua and Romeo San Andrés. He still owns Aging Room; Altadis distributes it but does not own it.</p>",
  "jochyblanco": "<p><strong>José Arnaldo \"Jochy\" Blanco III</strong> is CEO of <a href=\"/node/lapalma/\">Tabacalera Palma</a> in Tamboril, near Santiago, Dominican Republic. He is a fourth-generation tobacco grower and a second-generation cigar maker. His great-grandfather Manuel Blanco Lozada came to the Dominican Republic from Spain in the mid-1800s, and his father, José Arnaldo Blanco II, founded Tabacalera Palma in 1925.</p>\n<p>Palma sold only in the local market until 1980, when Jochy began making cigars for export. In 1995 he moved the factory to Tamboril's free-trade zone. He grows Criollo '98 and Piloto Cubano on farms in Santiago, La Canela, Gurabo, Jacagua and Mao, and still buys from growers his father worked with. Cigar Journal called him arguably the Dominican Republic's biggest independent grower and buyer of tobacco. His own brands are La Galera and La Instructora, the latter a collaboration with Aaron Saide. La Galera Maduro El Lector made Cigar Journal's Top 25 of 2017.</p>\n<p>Palma also makes cigars for other companies. Its best-known partnership is <a href=\"/node/agingroom/\">Aging Room</a> with <a href=\"/node/rafaelnodal/\">Rafael Nodal</a>'s <a href=\"/node/boutiqueblends/\">Boutique Blends</a>, where Blanco later became a partner alongside Rafael and Alina Nodal and Hank Bischoff. Early releases such as Bin No. 1 and Quattro F55 came from his factory. Cigar Journal also lists <a href=\"/node/lfd/\">La Flor Dominicana</a> and Villiger among the brands that have worked with him.</p>",
  "zayayounan": "<p><strong>Zaya S. Younan</strong> is the owner of <a href=\"/node/elseptimo/\">El Septimo</a>, the Costa Rican-made luxury cigar brand, which his Younan Collection bought in 2019. He is chairman and CEO of Younan Collection and Younan Company, an investment firm best known for real estate.</p>\n<p>The Dallas Morning News once called him the largest owner of office space in Dallas. The firm also owns hotels, châteaux, golf courses and Bordeaux vineyards in France. In March 2019 Younan Collection bought all the assets of Septimo Concept SA, the Geneva company behind El Septimo, and renamed it El Septimo Geneva SA.</p>\n<p>El Septimo was founded in 2005 by the French cigar distributor Gilles Botquin, who died in 2022. At the time of the sale it was made in Costa Rica at the Pure Aroma Cigars S.A. factory. Younan says his first El Septimo was a Double Shot from King Abdullah II of Jordan in 2018. He later reported the brand in more than 900 U.S. shops and about 2,000 worldwide. Under him the company has added larger ring gauges, starting with Gilgamesh in 2020, and themed collections such as Sacred Arts and the Emperor Collection.</p>\n<p>Younan did not found El Septimo. He is also unconnected to Costa Rica's other luxury house, <a href=\"/node/nelsonalfonso/\">Nelson Alfonso</a>'s <a href=\"/node/selectedtobacco/\">Selected Tobacco</a>, whose Atabey and Byron are rolled at <a href=\"/node/costaricatabacos/\">Tabacos de Costa Rica</a>.</p>",
};
